{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794586882,"nonce":"IuD44ika/2vuvBnBYnY1jjkjMbY=","message":"Slice 1 landed locally in a4e6f240 (not pushed).\n\n`ash --build --target aarch64-linux-android` (and the armv7, x86_64, i686 spellings) now links with the NDK alone:\n- The NDK is `ANDROID_NDK_HOME`/`ANDROID_NDK_ROOT`/`NDK_HOME`, else the newest under `ANDROID_HOME`/`ANDROID_SDK_ROOT`, else the newest under Android Studio's default SDK location (versions compared by number). The driver is the NDK's `\u003ctriple\u003e\u003capi\u003e-clang` wrapper; the API level is `ASH_ANDROID_API`, 24 when unset. A bare `CC` is ignored for these targets.\n- The program always links the static runtime, with `-ldl -lm` and `-Wl,-z,max-page-size=16384`.\n- std/build.rs takes the target's headers from the NDK sysroot, so building the runtime needs no `BINDGEN_EXTRA_CLANG_ARGS_*`.\n\nChecked: hello world and ten test programs, built only with `ash --build`, print what the interpreter prints on the arm64-v8a API 36 emulator, exit 0. `cargo test -p ash --test mobile_link` covers the link (skips with no NDK or runtime archive).\n\nOne thing for whoever builds the runtime: `cargo build -p ash_std --target aarch64-linux-android` also builds the cdylib, which needs the NDK as cargo's linker and fails with the host's. The staticlib alone is what the link uses: `cargo rustc -p ash_std --target aarch64-linux-android --release --crate-type staticlib`. The release workflow should build it that way.\n\nAcceptance items 1, 2 and 4 are met for the executable form; the loadable `.so` form is not. Still open: item 3 (runtimes in the release archives), item 5 (the project and entry glue), item 6 (`ash run`, CI), and static native binding. A physical device was attached during the last run; nothing was run on it.","files":null}]}